  "summary": "GPU.ai sells capacity on clouds it connects to and bills you itself, per second, at the rate its catalogue shows. That rate is the lowest it has live for the card at that minute across up to eight regions - its own words are \"lowest live on-demand rate per GPU, refreshed continuously\" - so it is a floor for the card and not the price of one machine you pick, and it can move between one hour and the next. Cards sold in two tiers carry two rates: the plain one, and the cheaper \"community\" supply the page marks as such.",
  "notes": "rendered page (the served HTML prints no figure at all); the \"Live platform rates\" table only, refused if its footnote or column labels are gone; every rate advertised_from; the tier word beside the card name is the market (community / on_demand), and a community rate equal to the plain one is the same figure twice and dropped; region null - the page names a count of regions, never a place; the catalogue reprices within the hour",
